WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.889
Assume a publishing application receives
thousands of reads for each edit. Sending all

00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:09.779
operations to one database makes the read
workload compete with the much smaller write

00:00:09.779 --> 00:00:14.668
workload. A primary with read replicas separates
them: the primary accepts changes and

00:00:14.668 --> 00:00:19.045
streams them to additional copies, while
ordinary reads can use those copies.

00:00:19.045 --> 00:00:24.003
The replicas are not independent authorities
allowed to invent conflicting versions. They

00:00:24.003 --> 00:00:29.017
reproduce the primary's history. This gives the
system more places to execute read queries

00:00:29.017 --> 00:00:33.417
without requiring every write to be decided
jointly by all application workers.

00:00:33.545 --> 00:00:38.779
It also reveals the limit: each replica still
needs to process the stream of changes, so

00:00:38.779 --> 00:00:42.169
replication does not divide the primary's write
workload.

00:00:42.169 --> 00:00:47.299
Walk through an edit followed immediately by a
refresh. The primary has revision 12, while

00:00:47.299 --> 00:00:52.373
one replica still has revision 11. A read routed
to that replica can return an apparently

00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:54.768
lost edit even though the write succeeded.

00:00:54.768 --> 00:00:59.848
The replica may be internally consistent with an
earlier point in the primary's history, yet

00:00:59.848 --> 00:01:04.874
it is stale relative to this user's action.
Possible policies include reading recent writes

00:01:04.874 --> 00:01:09.512
from the primary or waiting until a replica
reaches a required replication position.

00:01:09.512 --> 00:01:14.307
Those policies have latency and availability
costs. A label saying read replica is

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:19.510
incomplete unless the design also explains which
reads may tolerate lag and what the user

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:20.680
sees when lag grows.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:25.567
Replication puts copies of the same data on
several machines. Sharding instead assigns

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:30.455
different rows to different homes, allowing
independent writes to proceed on different

00:01:30.455 --> 00:01:35.571
machines. For a service organized around
accounts, an account identifier can determine
the

00:01:35.571 --> 00:01:37.674
shard holding that account's records.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.767
A request with that identifier can be routed
directly to its destination. The important

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:48.029
property is bounded participation: adding more
shards should not force an ordinary account

00:01:48.029 --> 00:01:53.234
request to involve more machines. Co-locating
related rows can also keep transactions and

00:01:53.234 --> 00:01:54.578
joins within one shard.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.706
This is why choosing a shard key is a decision
about the application's operations, not

00:01:59.706 --> 00:02:02.921
merely a way to divide a large table into equal
pieces.

00:02:02.921 --> 00:02:08.088
Now change the question from one account's
orders to all orders containing a particular

00:02:08.088 --> 00:02:13.254
product. If rows are partitioned by account, the
product identifier does not identify a

00:02:13.254 --> 00:02:18.243
single destination. The application may ask
every shard, combine results and sort or

00:02:18.243 --> 00:02:19.133
aggregate them.

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:24.105
That scatter-and-gather query grows more
expensive as shards are added and can wait for
the

00:02:24.105 --> 00:02:28.804
slowest participating shard. Dividing data can
therefore improve one access path while

00:02:28.804 --> 00:02:33.831
making another harder. This example needs both
paths: a direct arrow from account key to one

00:02:33.831 --> 00:02:37.218
shard and a branching arrow from a global query
to all shards.

00:02:37.218 --> 00:02:42.271
A secondary read model may improve the global
query, but it creates additional data to

00:02:42.271 --> 00:02:42.799
maintain.
